# Release a package

A release of package `PKG` is the annotated tag `PKG-v<version>` on `main`
(e.g. `[[ dist_name ]]-v1.0.0`) plus a GitHub Release. Nothing is published
to PyPI or Zenodo from this repository. Tagging and publishing are visible
to everyone with access: confirm the version with the user before step 2
and before step 4.

## 1. Check before releasing

```bash
git checkout main && git pull
just versions                 # current versions
just changelog PKG            # what the release will list
```

- The changelog only lists conventional commits that touched
  `packages/PKG/`. Missing entries mean commits without a conventional type
  or outside that path; edit the new `packages/PKG/CHANGELOG.md` section in the release
  commit or PR, which the draft release takes its notes from.
- A breaking change since the last release means a major bump (minor while
  the version is 0.x). Look for `feat!` / `BREAKING CHANGE`.
- `just canvodpy-ref`: the released package must work with canvodpy from
  PyPI, since an install from the tag ignores the root `[tool.uv.sources]`. If it
  needs code that is only on a canvodpy branch, release canvodpy first (see
  the `canvodpy-dependency` guide).

## 2. Release commit, on a branch

```bash
git checkout -b chore/release-PKG-X.Y.Z    # several packages: one branch
just release PKG X.Y.Z                     # or major | minor | patch
```

`just release` refuses a dirty tree or an existing tag, runs the package's
tests, then makes one commit `chore(release): PKG X.Y.Z` that sets the
version (package `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock`) and adds the new section at
the top of `packages/PKG/CHANGELOG.md`; earlier sections stay as they are. Several packages: run it once per package on
the same branch.

Check the commit: `git show --stat HEAD`, and read and edit the changelog
section; the draft release takes its notes from it.

## 3. PR and merge

```bash
git push --no-follow-tags -u origin chore/release-PKG-X.Y.Z
gh pr create --fill
```

Wait for CI (off here unless `ci_enabled` is true in `.copier-answers.yml`:
then run `just check && just test` instead) and the merge. Don't tag the branch: the merge commit differs
from the branch commit, and a tag on the branch would point outside `main`.

## 4. Tag

```bash
git checkout main && git pull
just tag PKG
```

`just tag` requires `main` equal to `origin/main` and the tag in the
changelog, then pushes the annotated tag. That runs
`.github/workflows/release.yml` (with the workflows off: start it by hand,
`gh workflow run release.yml --ref PKG-vX.Y.Z`), which checks the tag against the package
version and drafts a GitHub Release from the changelog.

## 5. Publish and verify

- `gh release list`, then read the draft (`gh release view PKG-vX.Y.Z`) and
  publish it: `gh release edit PKG-vX.Y.Z --draft=false`.
- Install from the tag outside the workspace:

```bash
uv venv /tmp/pkg-check && source /tmp/pkg-check/bin/activate
uv pip install "PKG @ git+[[ repo_url ]]@PKG-vX.Y.Z#subdirectory=packages/PKG"
python -c "import canvod.[[ module_name ]]"
```

## If something went wrong

- Wrong tag, not yet published: `git push --delete origin PKG-vX.Y.Z`,
  `git tag -d PKG-vX.Y.Z`, delete the draft, fix, tag again.
- Published release: never move its tag. Release a patch version instead.
